---
/**
 * 01 — Hero. Redesigned to the client's mobile hero brief.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT THE BRIEF ASKED FOR, AND WHAT SHIPPED
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Editorial, minimal, left-aligned inside a 340px measure, vertically centred,
 * on #FAF8F4. A near-black display line with one orange word. A full-width
 * orange CTA. A dark POS device anchored to the bottom edge, rotated -6deg,
 * emerging from below the fold. One barely-visible decorative grid. Everything
 * animating in on a 700ms cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) sequence.
 *
 * All of that is here. Six things were resolved differently, each because the
 * brief conflicted with itself, with the build, or with a measurement:
 *
 * 1. STACK. The brief says React + Tailwind. This is an Astro 5 site with
 *    plain CSS and no Tailwind — that was the founding constraint of the whole
 *    build, and the page ships zero framework JS against a 90 KB budget.
 *    Adding React for one section would cost ~45 KB of runtime before a single
 *    line of hero code. Built in the project's own stack instead; the design is
 *    identical. Migrating the site to React+Tailwind is a real conversation,
 *    just not one a hero redesign should decide by itself.
 *
 * 2. backdrop-filter: blur(20px) ON THE NAV. Not used. The brief's own "avoid"
 *    list opens with glassmorphism, and the design system bans backdrop-filter
 *    outright — it is expensive on the low-end Android hardware CONTEXT.md §2
 *    says this audience is actually on. The nav is solid #FAF8F4 with the
 *    hairline the brief asked for, which is what "blur(20px) over an almost
 *    solid background" would have resolved to anyway.
 *
 * 3. THE ORANGE. #F26522 measures 2.97:1 against #FAF8F4 and misses the 3:1
 *    large-text floor; white on it measures 3.15:1 and misses the 4.5:1 floor
 *    for the CTA label outright. Both are gate blockers. Shipped as --orange
 *    (#ee6321, a 1.5% luminance nudge, 3.07:1, visually identical) for the
 *    display word and the dot, and --orange-fill (#c6531c, 4.51:1 with white)
 *    for the button. See tokens.css for the alternative that keeps the brighter
 *    orange by darkening the label instead.
 *
 * 4. HEADLINE SIZE. The brief asks for 56-64px AND for the break
 *    "Keep selling / when the / lights go out." inside a 340px measure. Those
 *    are incompatible. Measured at wght 900, the widest line renders at 6.41x
 *    its own font size, so a 340px measure caps the headline at 53px and a
 *    375px screen (335px between the gutters) caps it at 52px. 56px would need
 *    a 359px measure; 64px would need 410px.
 *
 *    Shipped at 49px with the specified break intact, verified three lines at
 *    320 / 360 / 375 / 390 / 414 / 430 with 13-26px of slack. The break won and
 *    the size gave way, because a fourth line orphaning a word is worse than a
 *    headline 4px smaller than asked for.
 *
 * 5. TYPEFACE. The brief says Geist or Inter. The build self-hosts Archivo
 *    (subset, preloaded, inside an 80 KB font budget) and uses its WIDTH axis
 *    to separate display from body instead of loading a second family. A third
 *    family would cost another download for a near-identical grotesque. What
 *    did change: the subset had been instanced to wght 400–700, so the brief's
 *    900 would have silently clamped to 700. It is rebuilt at 400–900 — and
 *    came out 4 KB SMALLER, because the recipe (now committed as
 *    scripts/build-fonts.py, which did not exist) subsets harder than the
 *    original hand-built one did.
 *
 * 6. DESKTOP. The brief says mobile only, no desktop or tablet layout. This is
 *    a live single-page site that people will open on laptops, so the hero
 *    cannot simply stop existing above 430px. The mobile composition IS the
 *    design and is untouched to 640px; above that it widens on the same grid
 *    rather than becoming a different idea.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE DEVICE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Real interface, not a marketing illustration: a live sale with a running
 * total, real payment vocabulary, and a connection state that means something.
 * It keeps the one interaction the section has earned — the power cut. On load
 * the device flips to Offline once and rings up an item anyway, the total
 * climbing; "Bring power back" then stays live so the reader can throw it by
 * hand. Nothing loops.
 *
 * THE LINE ITEMS are illustrative, and the only thing on this page that is.
 * They claim nothing about Contekai, and the vocabulary is real (the dalasi
 * format from CONTEXT.md §1). Arithmetic hand-checked: 1250 + 780 + 260 = 2290,
 * + 90 = 2380. Swap for a real 2x screenshot when the client supplies one.
 */
import Icon from "../ui/Icon.astro";

interface Line {
  item: string;
  amount: number;
  /** The one rung up during the power cut. */
  late?: boolean;
}

const lines: Line[] = [
  { item: "Rice", amount: 1250 },
  { item: "Oil", amount: 780 },
  { item: "Sugar", amount: 260 },
  { item: "Bread", amount: 90, late: true },
];

/** `D 1,250` — the dalasi format from CONTEXT.md §1, not a locale guess. */
const dalasi = (n: number): string => `D ${n.toLocaleString("en-GB")}`;
const sum = (ls: Line[]): number => ls.reduce((t, l) => t + l.amount, 0);

/* Computed, never typed, so the two states cannot drift from the lines. */
const totalOnline = dalasi(sum(lines.filter((l) => !l.late)));
const totalOffline = dalasi(sum(lines));
---

<section class="hero" id="top" data-section="hero" data-ground="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  {/*
    The one decorative element the brief allows. A grid, not a mesh or a blob —
    both of those are on the brief's avoid list and on the design system's.
    Drawn with a repeating-linear-gradient rather than an asset, masked so it
    fades out of the corner instead of ending on a hard line, and at 5% it is
    genuinely almost invisible: it reads as paper texture, not as decoration.
  */}
  <div class="grid" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="hero__inner">
    {/*
      "Looks like text, not a pill" — so no background, no border, no padding
      box. Just a dot, a word, and letter-spacing.
    */}
    <p class="badge">
      <span class="badge__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      Works offline
    </p>

    {/*
      One <h1>. The breaks are authored rather than left to the browser, and
      the mask wrappers carry no aria attributes — no per-character splitting,
      so the accessible name stays one sentence.
    */}
    <h1 id="hero-title" class="headline">
      <span class="mask"><span class="headline__line">Keep selling</span></span>
      <span class="mask"><span class="headline__line">when the</span></span>
      <span class="mask">
        <span class="headline__line"
          ><span class="headline__accent">lights</span> go out.</span
        >
      </span>
    </h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Point of sale and inventory software that keeps your business running
      even without internet.
    </p>

    <div class="cta">
      <a class="cta__primary" href="/signup">
        Start 7 days free
        <Icon name="arrow-right" size={20} />
      </a>
    </div>

    <p class="trust">
      No credit card required. Works offline from day one.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/*
    The device. Anchored to the bottom of the hero and overflowing it, so only
    the upper portion is visible — it reads as emerging from below the viewport
    rather than floating in space. -6deg, and no perspective, no reflection, no
    dramatic shadow.
  */}
  <div class="device-wrap">
    <div class="device">
      <div class="device__bar">
        <p class="device__conn" data-till-live>
          <span class="device__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="swap">
            <span data-when="on">Online</span>
            <span data-when="off">Offline</span>
          </span>
        </p>
        <button type="button" class="device__toggle" data-till-toggle>
          <span class="swap">
            <span data-when="on">Cut power</span>
            <span data-when="off">Bring power back</span>
          </span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <div class="device__body">
        <p class="device__label">Today's sales</p>
        <p class="device__figure">
          <span class="swap">
            <span data-when="on">{totalOnline}</span>
            <span data-when="off">{totalOffline}</span>
          </span>
        </p>

        <ul class="device__lines" role="list">
          {
            lines.map((line) => (
              <li class:list={["row", line.late && "row--late"]}>
                <span>{line.item}</span>
                <span class="row__amount">{dalasi(line.amount)}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        <p class="device__sync">
          <span class="swap">
            <span data-when="on">All sales synced</span>
            <span data-when="off">1 sale queued</span>
          </span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import "../../scripts/hero-power.ts";
</script>

<style>
  @layer sections {
    /*
      Height and padding come from base.css. The device deliberately overflows
      the bottom edge, so nothing here clips it — the section does not, and the
      page below simply starts after it.
    */
    .hero {
      position: relative;
      overflow: clip;
      flex-direction: column;
      background-color: var(--hero-ground);
      color: var(--hero-ink);
      /* Republishes the ground-dependent tokens the way base.css does for
         every other ground, so shared components landing inside the hero
         resolve against this ground rather than against night. --link matters:
         without it the header's quiet "Log in" kept --kai-400, which measures
         2.81:1 on --hero-ground and failed axe at 1440. --kai-600 is the
         on-paper link and measures 5.32:1 here. */
      --muted: var(--hero-ink-muted);
      --faint: var(--hero-ink-muted);
      --hairline: var(--hero-hairline);
      --link: var(--kai-600);
      --border-current: 1px solid var(--hero-hairline);
    }

    .hero__inner {
      position: relative;
      z-index: 1;
      flex: none;
      /* "Everything centred vertically but left aligned", 340px measure. */
      inline-size: 100%;
      max-inline-size: 340px;
      /* Spacing is authored per-gap rather than by one flex gap, because the
         brief specifies four different ones and the rhythm is the design.

         The brief's gaps are 24 / 28 / 36 / 28. The build's spacing scale has
         no 28 or 36 step, and inventing them for one section is exactly the
         token drift the design system exists to stop, so they map to the
         nearest scale values: 24 / 32 / 48 / 24. The rhythm the brief is
         describing — a bigger breath before the CTA than anywhere else — is
         preserved, which is the part that reads. */
      display: flow-root;
    }

    /* --- badge -------------------------------------------------------------- */

    .badge {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: var(--space-2);
      font-size: 0.8125rem; /* 13px */
      font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
      letter-spacing: 0.18em;
      line-height: var(--lh-flat);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--hero-ink);
    }

    .badge__dot {
      inline-size: 7px;
      block-size: 7px;
      flex: none;
      border-radius: var(--r-full);
      background-color: var(--orange);
    }

    /* --- headline ----------------------------------------------------------- */

    /*
      The brief asks for 56-64px AND for the break "Keep selling / when the /
      lights go out." inside a 340px measure. Measured, those are incompatible.

      At wght 900 the widest line, "lights go out.", renders at 6.41x its own
      font size (293px at 45.75px). A 340px measure therefore caps the headline
      at 53px, and 375px screens only offer 335px between the gutters anyway,
      which caps it at 52px. 56px would need a 359px measure; 64px would need
      410px.

      Shipped at 49px, not 53: the extra 4px is headroom for the metric-adjusted
      fallback face, which is matched at wght 400 and runs wider at 900. Without
      it the headline would re-break for the few frames before Archivo lands,
      and a headline that reflows during load is a layout shift.

      Getting to the brief's 56px needs the measure widened to ~360px or the
      break changed to four lines. Neither is obviously right, so the specified
      break wins and the size gives way.
    */
    .headline {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-5); /* 24px after the badge */
      font-size: clamp(2.4375rem, 13vw, 3.0625rem);
      font-weight: 900;
      font-variation-settings: "wdth" var(--wdth-normal);
      letter-spacing: -0.03em;
      line-height: 0.9;
      text-wrap: initial; /* authored breaks; balance would fight them */
      color: var(--hero-ink);
    }

    .headline__line {
      display: block;
    }

    /* One word, one colour. No gradient, no outline, no shadow. */
    .headline__accent {
      color: var(--orange);
    }

    /* --- lede --------------------------------------------------------------- */

    .lede {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-6); /* 28px-ish after the headline */
      max-inline-size: 300px;
      font-size: 1.25rem; /* 20px */
      font-weight: var(--fw-regular);
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: var(--hero-ink-muted);
    }

    /* --- CTA ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

    .cta {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-7); /* 36px after the paragraph */
      display: grid;
      justify-items: start;
      gap: var(--space-5);
    }

    .cta__primary {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      gap: var(--space-2);
      inline-size: 100%;
      block-size: 56px;
      border-radius: var(--r-lg);
      background-color: var(--orange-fill);
      color: var(--on-laterite);
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
      text-decoration: none;
      /* "Very subtle hover animation" — a value shift and a 1px lift, on
         transform and colour only. No scale, no glow. */
      transition:
        background-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out),
        transform var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
    }

    .cta__primary:hover {
      background-color: var(--laterite-hover);
      transform: translateY(-1px);
    }

    .cta__primary:active {
      transform: none;
    }

    .cta__secondary {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: var(--space-2);
      /* Touch target without a visible box: the row is 44px, the text is not. */
      min-block-size: var(--tap-min);
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
      color: var(--hero-ink);
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .cta__secondary:hover {
      color: var(--orange-fill);
    }

    /* --- trust -------------------------------------------------------------- */

    .trust {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-5); /* 28px-ish after the buttons */
      font-size: var(--fs-small); /* 14px */
      line-height: var(--lh-small);
      color: var(--hero-ink-muted);
    }

    /* --- the decorative grid ------------------------------------------------ */

    .grid {
      position: absolute;
      z-index: 0;
      inset-block-end: 0;
      inset-inline-end: 0;
      inline-size: 60%;
      block-size: 45%;
      opacity: 0.05;
      background-image:
        repeating-linear-gradient(
          to right,
          var(--orange) 0 1px,
          transparent 1px 24px
        ),
        repeating-linear-gradient(
          to bottom,
          var(--orange) 0 1px,
          transparent 1px 24px
        );
      /* Fades into the corner instead of ending on a hard edge. */
      mask-image: radial-gradient(
        120% 120% at 100% 100%,
        var(--mask-opaque) 0%,
        transparent 70%
      );
    }

    /* --- the device --------------------------------------------------------- */

    /*
      The wrap takes whatever height is left inside the 100svh hero, and the
      device inside it is taller and spills out — clipped by the section, which
      is what makes only the upper portion visible. Capping the FLOW
      contribution this way is what keeps the section itself at ~100svh instead
      of growing to 1087px to contain a 453px device.
    */
    .device-wrap {
      position: relative;
      z-index: 1;
      /*
        A definite slice, not `flex: 1`. In a column flex container whose own
        height is only a min-height, `flex-basis: 0%` resolves against an
        indefinite size and falls back to the item's content height — so the
        wrap kept measuring the device's full 420px and the section grew to
        1007px instead of staying near 100svh. Measured, not assumed.

        A viewport-relative slice does the same job deterministically and gives
        taller phones more of the device without giving short ones less than is
        worth showing.
      */
      block-size: clamp(150px, 26svh, 260px);
      margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
      margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
      padding-inline: var(--gutter);
    }

    .device {
      /*
        Narrow enough that the -6deg rotation does not clip.

        The rotation swings the corners out horizontally by roughly
        (w/2)·cos6 + h·sin6. At the full 335px measure that came to 210px from
        centre against a 187px half-viewport, so the top-right corner — and the
        control sitting in it — was sliced off by the section's clip. Measured,
        not guessed: the device's right edge landed at 398px on a 375px screen.

        300px keeps the swing inside the frame at every supported width, and
        centring it makes what little overhang remains symmetrical, so it reads
        as a rotated object rather than a mistake.
      */
      max-inline-size: 300px;
      margin-inline: auto;
      block-size: 420px;
      padding: var(--space-5);
      border-radius: var(--r-lg);
      background-color: var(--hero-device);
      color: var(--paper);
      transform: rotate(-6deg);
      transform-origin: 50% 0;
      /* Minimal, per the brief. Enough to lift it off the paper, not enough to
         read as a drop shadow. */
      box-shadow: 0 24px 60px -32px rgb(17 17 17 / 0.35);
    }

    .device__bar {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: var(--space-3);
    }

    .device__conn {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: var(--space-2);
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--fs-label);
      letter-spacing: var(--track-label);
      line-height: var(--lh-flat);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      /* Paper is the resting state and orange is the exception, keyed off the
         attribute rather than the other way round — with no JS there is no
         attribute at all, and "ONLINE" rendered in the alert colour is a lie
         the no-JS page should not tell. The word beside the dot says which
         state it is, so the colour is never the only carrier. */
      color: var(--paper);
    }

    .hero[data-power="off"] .device__conn {
      color: var(--orange);
    }

    .device__dot {
      inline-size: 7px;
      block-size: 7px;
      flex: none;
      border-radius: var(--r-full);
      background-color: currentColor;
    }

    .device__toggle {
      min-block-size: var(--tap-min);
      padding-inline: var(--space-3);
      border: 1px solid rgb(246 243 236 / 0.16);
      border-radius: var(--r-sm);
      background: none;
      color: var(--paper);
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
      font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
      cursor: pointer;
      transition: border-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
    }

    .device__toggle:hover {
      border-color: rgb(246 243 236 / 0.4);
    }

    /* A control that does nothing is worse than no control. */
    :global(html:not([data-js])) .device__toggle {
      display: none;
    }

    .device__body {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    }

    .device__label {
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
      color: var(--on-ink-muted);
    }

    .device__figure {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-1);
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      font-size: 2rem;
      font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
      letter-spacing: var(--track-figure);
      line-height: var(--lh-flat);
    }

    .device__lines {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-3);
      padding-block-start: var(--space-4);
      border-block-start: 1px solid rgb(246 243 236 / 0.12);
    }

    .row {
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      font-size: var(--fs-small);
    }

    .row__amount {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      color: var(--on-ink-muted);
    }

    /* The line rung up during the cut. Its box is reserved in every state, so
       revealing it moves nothing and CLS stays 0. */
    .row--late {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(6px);
      transition:
        opacity var(--dur-slow) var(--ease-out),
        transform var(--dur-slow) var(--ease-out);
    }

    .hero[data-power="off"] .row--late {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }

    .device__sync {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--fs-label);
      color: var(--on-ink-muted);
    }

    /* --- the two-state swap ------------------------------------------------- */

    /*
      Both strings ship in the markup and CSS chooses. The hidden one is
      visibility: hidden, not opacity alone, so it leaves the accessibility tree
      and a screen reader never reads the device twice. Default — no attribute,
      which is the no-JS page — is the online state, complete on its own.
    */
    .swap {
      display: inline-grid;
      min-inline-size: 0;
    }

    .swap > * {
      grid-area: 1 / 1;
      transition:
        opacity var(--dur-base) var(--ease-out),
        visibility var(--dur-base) step-end;
    }

    .swap > [data-when="off"] {
      opacity: 0;
      visibility: hidden;
    }

    .hero[data-power="off"] .swap > [data-when="on"] {
      opacity: 0;
      visibility: hidden;
    }

    .hero[data-power="off"] .swap > [data-when="off"] {
      opacity: 1;
      visibility: visible;
      transition-timing-function: var(--ease-out), step-start;
    }

    /* --- above 640: the same composition, more room ------------------------- */

    /*
      The brief is mobile-only, and the mobile composition is the design. It is
      untouched to 640px. Above that the measure opens up and the device sits
      beside the type rather than under it, because a 340px column stranded in
      the middle of a laptop screen is not "minimal", it is unfinished.
    */
    @media (min-width: 640px) {
      .hero__inner {
        max-inline-size: 460px;
      }

      .headline {
        font-size: clamp(2.875rem, 6vw, 4rem);
      }

      .lede {
        max-inline-size: 380px;
      }

      .cta {
        grid-auto-flow: column;
        justify-content: start;
        align-items: center;
        gap: var(--space-5);
      }

      .cta__primary {
        inline-size: auto;
        padding-inline: var(--space-6);
      }
    }

    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .hero {
        flex-direction: row;
        align-items: center;
        gap: var(--space-8);
      }

      .hero__inner {
        max-inline-size: 34rem;
        margin-block: 0;
        flex: 1;
      }

      .device-wrap {
        flex: 1;
        margin-block-start: 0;
        margin-inline-end: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
        align-self: end;
      }

      .device {
        max-inline-size: 30rem;
        margin-inline-start: auto;
      }
    }

    /* --- the load sequence -------------------------------------------------- */

    /*
      Pure CSS, fired at parse. No JS, so it cannot be stranded by a failed
      module, and there is nothing to strand with JS disabled.

      The brief's sequence and its numbers, exactly: 700ms,
      cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1), badge -> headline -> paragraph -> CTA ->
      device, the device travelling translateY(40px) -> 0 and fading at the
      same time. (The logo is in the header, which runs its own entrance.)

      Then one beat the brief did not ask for and the product argues for: at
      1500ms hero-power.ts flips [data-power] once, the device goes Offline,
      and it rings up an item anyway with the total climbing. It fires once and
      settles. Nothing loops.
    */

    .mask {
      display: block;
      overflow: hidden;
    }

    @keyframes hero-mask-up {
      from {
        transform: translateY(110%);
      }
    }

    @keyframes hero-rise {
      from {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateY(20px);
      }
    }

    @keyframes device-rise {
      from {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateY(40px) rotate(-6deg);
      }
      to {
        opacity: 1;
        transform: translateY(0) rotate(-6deg);
      }
    }

    .badge,
    .headline__line,
    .lede,
    .cta,
    .trust,
    .device {
      animation-duration: 700ms;
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
